parts.text:
Confirmed: there's **no list page for assessments** and no loading of existing assessments when selecting a patient. The page `/app/protocolos-aba/assessment` only:
1. Shows the form
2. Loads an assessment only if URL has an id (`assessment/:id`)

So when the user selects Theo, nothing appears — the evaluations don't load, no list of saved assessments exists.

The fix: add loading of the existing assessment when a patient is selected (on patient select change, fetch the latest assessment for that patient+protocol and load its evaluations). Also maybe show a list of saved assessments.

Let me add:
1. In ngOnInit, subscribe to `selectedPatientId` changes (or handle in the select's change event) → fetch `GET /aba/assessments?patientId=X` → if exists, load the latest (per protocol? or the latest overall).
2. Also display which assessments exist — maybe a small dropdown/badge showing saved assessments for the patient.

Simplest UX that matches expectations: when patient changes, fetch assessments list; if there's an assessment for the currently selected protocol, load its evaluations into the form (so scores/radar appear); otherwise start empty (new assessment).

Let me implement:

```ts
@ViewChild('radarChart') ...
savedAssessments = signal<any[]>([]);
currentAssessmentId = signal('');
isEdit — hmm, but isEdit is used for save (PUT vs POST). If we load an assessment for the selected patient, saving should PUT to that id. I'll track loadedAssessmentId.

loadAssessmentsForPatient() {
  const pid = this.selectedPatientId();
  if (!pid) { this.savedAssessments.set([]); return; }
  this.api.get(`/aba/assessments`, { patientId: pid }).subscribe((res: any) => {
    this.savedAssessments.set(res.data || []);
    const match = (res.data || []).find((a: any) => a.protocolType === this.selectedProtocol());
    if (match) {
      this.loadEvaluationIntoForm(match);
    } else {
      // keep empty
      this.evaluations.set({});
      this.notes = '';
      this.loadedAssessmentId.set('');
    }
  });
}
```

But careful with the existing `effect` that resets evaluations on protocol change (line 261-269): when protocol changes, evaluations reset and domain reselect. If patient has an assessment for that protocol, we should load it. The effect runs on selectedProtocol change. So inside the effect, after resetting, fetch the assessment for the new protocol.

But there's a subtlety: when the user changes the patient via the select, `selectedPatientId` changes — we should also load. Let me restructure:

1. On patient select change: `onPatientChange()` → fetch assessments → load for current protocol.
2. In effect (protocol change): after reset, fetch assessments for the current patient (if any) → load matching protocol.

Loading into form:
```ts
loadEvaluationIntoForm(a: any) {
  this.loadedAssessmentId.set(a.id);
  this.assessmentDate = a.assessedAt ? new Date(a.assessedAt).toISOString().split('T')[0] : ...;
  this.notes = a.notes || '';
  if (a.evaluations) { try { this.evaluations.set(JSON.parse(a.evaluations)); } catch { this.evaluations.set({}); } }
}
```

And save(): use PUT if `this.loadedAssessmentId()` else POST (for the selected protocol). Note: existing `isEdit` is URL-driven (assessment/:id). If I PUT to the loaded assessment id... but careful: a patient could have assessments in all 3 protocols; when user switches protocol, we should PUT the specific protocol's assessment id.

Edge case: user edits an assessment loaded from patient select → then switches protocol → loads the other protocol's assessment → saves → PUT to that id. That works with `loadedAssessmentId`.

But: if loadedAssessmentId is set, the POST vs PUT decision changes — `const req = this.isEdit || this.loadedAssessmentId() ? put : post`.

Also add a small UI showing saved assessments: e.g., under the protocol tabs, a list of "Avaliações salvas" for the patient with dates/score, clicking loads it. That might be overkill but useful. Let me keep it minimal but helpful: show a small bar with chips of saved assessments for the patient: "ABLLS-R 156 pts (17/08)" etc., clickable to load. Actually let me keep the scope tight: auto-load on patient/protocol selection + a subtle indicator of the loaded assessment date.

Hmm, but the user's complaint is "no frontend não apareceu" — they expect the data to appear. Auto-loading when selecting the patient + protocol is exactly what fixes it. I'll also add a small saved-assessments row so they can see all 3 exist and jump between them.

Let me look at how the effect interacts. Current effect:

```ts
effect(() => {
  this.selectedProtocol();
  this.evaluations.set({});
  this.selectedDomain.set(null);
  setTimeout(() => {
    const domains = this.currentDomains();
    if (domains.length > 0) this.selectDomain(domains[0]);
  }, 50);
});
```

Problem: this effect resets evaluations whenever protocol changes — even when the user just loaded an assessment and then switches protocol and back. That's existing behavior; fine.

I'll modify the effect to also reload the assessment for the new protocol:

```ts
effect(() => {
  this.selectedProtocol();
  this.evaluations.set({});
  this.selectedDomain.set(null);
  this.loadAssessmentForProtocol();
  setTimeout(() => {
    const domains = this.currentDomains();
    if (domains.length > 0) this.selectDomain(domains[0]);
  }, 50);
});
```

And patient select gets (change)="onPatientChange()" — actually with [(ngModel)] on a signal... hmm. `[(ngModel)]="selectedPatientId"` — does that even work with signals? In Angular 18, ngModel with a signal writes via signal setter? Signals support direct assignment `x = value`? No — but the ngModel directive writes to the bound expression... With a signal, `[(ngModel)]` binds to the signal's `set` method via the "two-way binding to signal" feature added in Angular 17.2? Actually yes — Angular supports `[(ngModel)]` on signals? I recall ngModel doesn't officially support signal two-way binding... Actually in Angular 17.2+, `[(ngModel)]` with a signal IS supported (the signal's `.set` is invoked). Hmm, let me check the docs memory: Angular 17.2 added "two-way binding to signals" for template-driven forms... I'm not 100% sure. But the existing code uses it and presumably works (the previous assessments were saved with patient Gabriel via this form, per notes session 06/08). So it works.

For patient change, I'll use `(change)="onPatientChange()"` on the select element plus load in ngOnInit after patients load? Simpler: add `(change)` handler. Actually cleaner: in `ngOnInit`, after patients load, don't auto-select. Keep the handler.

Let me write the changes:

1. Template: add `(change)="onPatientChange()"` to the patient select.
2. Add saved assessments chips UI — where? Under the protocol tabs row. Keep it compact: if savedAssessments().length > 0 show a card row with buttons. Each chip: protocol name + total score + date, click → load that assessment (set protocol + load).

Actually, to keep the change focused and avoid UI clutter, I'll add a small card above the domains grid showing "Avaliações salvas do paciente" with chips. It makes testing visible.

Hmm, wait — actually there's a subtlety with protocol switching effect: the effect will call loadAssessmentForProtocol() which fetches. But onPatientChange also fetches. Both set evaluations. Fine.

Also careful: `loadAssessment` (URL edit mode) also exists. If isEdit, we don't need onPatientChange behavior — the URL load sets patient, protocol, evaluations. But then the effect on protocol change would reload and override the loaded data! Wait — the effect tracks selectedProtocol(); when loadAssessment sets selectedProtocol.set(res.protocolType), the effect fires, resets evaluations, and calls my new loadAssessmentForProtocol() which would fetch assessments for the patient and re-load the same data (the same assessment). That's fine actually — it re-loads identical data. But assessmentDate/notes would be reset by the effect... My loadAssessmentForProtocol should set notes and date from the fetched assessment too. OK.

But hmm — there's a risk: in edit mode the assessment loaded is for patient X protocol ABLLS-R; loadAssessmentForProtocol fetches by patientId → finds the same assessment → reloads same values. Fine. If the patient has multiple assessments, loads the one matching current protocol. Fine.

Edge: user creates NEW assessment (empty patient or patient with no assessment) — loadAssessmentForProtocol returns nothing → keeps empty. Good.

Implementation details:

```ts
loadedAssessmentId = signal('');
savedAssessments = signal<any[]>([]);

onPatientChange() {
  this.loadSavedAssessments();
}

loadSavedAssessments() {
  const pid = this.selectedPatientId();
  if (!pid) { this.savedAssessments.set([]); this.evaluations.set({}); this.notes = ''; this.loadedAssessmentId.set(''); return; }
  this.api.get('/aba/assessments', { patientId: pid }).subscribe({
    next: (res: any) => {
      const list = res.data || [];
      this.savedAssessments.set(list);
      this.loadAssessmentForProtocol(list);
    }
  });
}

loadAssessmentForProtocol(list?: any[]) {
  const pid = this.selectedPatientId();
  if (!pid) { return; }
  if (!list) {
    this.api.get('/aba/assessments', { patientId: pid }).subscribe((res: any) => this.applyAssessment((res.data || []).find((a: any) => a.protocolType === this.selectedProtocol())));
  } else {
    this.applyAssessment(list.find((a: any) => a.protocolType === this.selectedProtocol()));
  }
}

applyAssessment(a: any) {
  if (a) {
    this.loadedAssessmentId.set(a.id);
    this.assessmentDate = a.assessedAt ? new Date(a.assessedAt).toISOString().split('T')[0] : this.assessmentDate;
    this.notes = a.notes || '';
    try { this.evaluations.set(JSON.parse(a.evaluations)); } catch { this.evaluations.set({}); }
  } else {
    this.loadedAssessmentId.set('');
  }
}

loadAssessmentById(id: string) {
  const a = this.savedAssessments().find(x => x.id === id);
  if (a) {
    this.selectedProtocol.set(a.protocolType as ProtocolType);
    this.applyAssessment(a);
    setTimeout(() => { const d = this.currentDomains(); if (d.length) this.selectDomain(d[0]); }, 50);
  }
}
```

And the effect becomes:

```ts
effect(() => {
  this.selectedProtocol();
  this.evaluations.set({});
  this.selectedDomain.set(null);
  this.loadAssessmentForProtocol();
  setTimeout(() => {
    const domains = this.currentDomains();
    if (domains.length > 0) this.selectDomain(domains[0]);
  }, 50);
});
```

Wait but there's a race: effect fires on protocol change before savedAssessments is loaded (if patient selected but no fetch yet)? onPatientChange loads. Effect calls loadAssessmentForProtocol() which fetches if list not provided. OK but double-fetch: onPatientChange fetches list and calls loadAssessmentForProtocol(list). The effect only fires on protocol change; if protocol hasn't changed, no extra fetch. Good.

Hmm, one more concern: in the effect, when user switches protocol, `applyAssessment` loads the other protocol's data — good, that's desired.

Save: 
```ts
const req = this.isEdit
  ? this.api.put(`/aba/assessments/${this.assessmentId}`, data)
  : this.loadedAssessmentId()
    ? this.api.put(`/aba/assessments/${this.loadedAssessmentId()}`, data)
    : this.api.post('/aba/assessments', data);
```

Hmm wait — but there's a subtle problem: if the user selects a patient with a saved ABLLS-R assessment, edits it, then switches to VB-MAPP (no assessment) and saves — POST creates VB-MAPP. Then switches back to ABLLS-R, edits, saves → PUT updates ABLLS-R. All consistent because loadedAssessmentId always reflects the current protocol's assessment id.

But one problem: `applyAssessment` on patient change might override `assessmentDate` even when the user picked a date first. Minor; acceptable.

Also after save success, refresh savedAssessments list. I'll call loadSavedAssessments() in the next handler before navigating... it navigates away after 1.5s anyway. Keep simple: in next, refresh silently (the page navigates to list anyway).

UI: add under protocol tabs a row of chips when savedAssessments().length > 0:

```html
@if (savedAssessments().length > 0) {
  <div class="bg-white dark:bg-slate-900 rounded-2xl p-4 shadow-sm ring-1 ring-slate-200 dark:ring-slate-800 flex flex-wrap items-center gap-2">
    <span class="text-xs font-bold text-slate-500 uppercase tracking-widest">Avaliações salvas:</span>
    @for (a of savedAssessments(); track a.id) {
      <button (click)="loadAssessmentById(a.id)"
        class="flex items-center gap-2 px-3 py-1.5 rounded-xl text-xs font-bold transition-all"
        [class]="loadedAssessmentId() === a.id ? 'bg-primary text-on-primary' : 'bg-slate-100 dark:bg-slate-800 text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-300 hover:bg-slate-200 dark:hover:bg-slate-700'">
        {{ a.protocolType }} · {{ a.totalScore }} pts · {{ a.assessedAt ? (a.assessedAt | date:'dd/MM') : '' }}
      </button>
    }
  </div>
}
```

Uses the date pipe — CommonModule is imported, so `| date` works.

Note: in edit mode (URL :id), onPatientChange isn't triggered; but savedAssessments will be empty, chips not shown. loadAssessment (URL) sets patient; maybe also load saved list? Not necessary.

Wait — one more important detail: in edit mode via URL, after loadAssessment sets protocol, the effect fires loadAssessmentForProtocol which fetches by patientId — will find the same assessment (matching protocol) and re-apply — including setting loadedAssessmentId. But `this.assessmentId` (URL) is used for PUT in edit mode — same id anyway. OK.

Also `getScore()` returns -1 for unset; fine.

Also — validation: patient select `(change)` handler. Add it.

One risk: effect + loadAssessmentForProtocol() calling this.api.get each protocol switch — fine, small.

Also careful about strict mode compile: `list?: any[]` param. OK.

Let me now apply edits.

Actually — wait. Let me reconsider the effect. The effect currently ALSO fires when selectedProtocol changes at loadAssessment() during URL edit... and the effect body resets evaluations then reloads via fetch — async, so for a moment empty, then filled. selectDomain called in setTimeout 50ms — same as before. OK.

Another subtlety: `effect` runs in constructor-context; calling api.get inside is fine.

Also initial load: when page opens (no patient selected), effect fires (protocol ABLLS-R) → loadAssessmentForProtocol() → selectedPatientId() is '' → return early. Good, no fetch.

Now, in onPatientChange — also need to reset domain selection? applyAssessment sets evaluations; the domain sidebar counts (getDomainScore) are computed from evaluations signal, reactive. selectedDomain stays as-is. Good.

Edge: patient switches from Theo to another patient without assessment → savedAssessments empty → evaluations reset to {}. applyAssessment(undefined) → loadedAssessmentId '' but evaluations remain from previous patient! Need to reset evaluations when no assessment found. In applyAssessment else branch: this.evaluations.set({}); this.notes = ''.

Let me write the final code.

